// Herd animals: where each lived wild on the real Earth, the climate it tolerates, and what it gives.
//
// Climate ranges work as for crops (crops.json): four numbers; below the first the animal cannot
// be kept, from the second to the third it does best, above the fourth it cannot be kept.
//   warmest  mean temperature of the warmest month, degrees C
//   coldest  mean temperature of the coldest month, degrees C
//   water    rainfall over the year plus what a river adds (farming.json), mm
// An animal can have more than one homeland, each a circle on the real Earth; it can first be
// herded in each of them separately (cattle in Southwest Asia and in the eastern Sahel).
//   afterFarmingYears  it is first herded in this homeland only where farming has stood in the
//                      cell or a neighbour at least this long (milestone 3): animals were first
//                      kept by settled farmers, sheep and goats about a thousand years after the
//                      first farming in Southwest Asia, llamas as Andean farming took hold. Sheep,
//                      goats and Southwest Asian cattle wait 300 years: farming starts there later
//                      in some runs, and at 500 they came as late as 5800 BCE.
//   foragers           foragers on pasture far from farmers may also first herd it, less readily
//                      (herding.json "foragerInventionFactor"): eastern Sahel cattle may have been
//                      kept before farming came there.
//   tamedBy            a technology (technologies.json) through which it comes to be kept, in place
//                      of being first herded by chance: horses, kept where people know horse herding
//                      and horses live wild, then spreading with their herders and to farmers.
//   herded  true: kept by herders on open pasture (herding.json); false: kept only by farmers
//           around their villages (pigs), and first taken up by farmers.
//   wool    whether it gives wool; every animal gives meat, milk and hides.
